  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry conditions with above normal temperatures persist through \nthe weekend. This will exacerbate ongoing water resource drought\nconditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nDeep layered ridging builds through today and continues\nThursday. Keeping the area dry through Thursday due to the\nsubsidence under the ridge.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThere may be a chance for some precipitation early next week \nbefore high pressure returns. Any substantial drought relief is\nunlikely.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn upper level trough will dig into the northeast Monday,\nswinging a cold front across the region, at the same time as a \ncoastal low moves up the east coast. How these two features \ninteract will be the primary factor that determines rainfall \nvalues across the region. Timing and spatial uncertainty \npersists in the latest guidance. Some models are more rapidly \npushing the lingering high pressure out of the region. This \nwould allow the coastal system to more rapidly track across the \nGulf of Maine. If the system can more rapidly track north along \nthe East Coast, it will arrive ahead of the cold front, allowing\na more coastal track along Downeast Maine. Both these \nuncertainties will determine the amount of rainfall, \nparticularly along coastal regions.\n\nFollowing the passage of the front, there is strong agreement \namong ensembles that Canadian high pressure will move in, \nbringing cold and dry weather through Black Friday.",
